# =============================================================================
#  LolSuit - PRODUCTION configuration  (one EC2 instance + Amazon RDS)
# =============================================================================
#  This lives on the EC2 instance, NOT in git. On the box:
#
#      cd /opt/lolsuit/prod
#      cp .env.example .env
#      chmod 600 .env          # it holds your RDS password
#      nano .env
#
#  Unlike the repo root's .env.example, most of these have NO default. The prod
#  compose file declares them `${VAR:?...}`, so a missing value aborts the
#  deploy instead of quietly booting production with a development password.
#  An unset secret should cost you a failed `up`, not a breach.
# =============================================================================

# --- which images to run ------------------------------------------------------
# Your Docker Hub account (or organisation). Images are pulled as
# <DOCKERHUB_USERNAME>/lolsuit-server and <DOCKERHUB_USERNAME>/lolsuit-web.
DOCKERHUB_USERNAME=your-dockerhub-username

# The version to run. deploy.sh rewrites this line on every deploy, so the file
# is always an honest record of what is live - and the previous value is what
# a rollback returns to.
#
# Pin a real version. NEVER `latest`: it makes "what is running?" unanswerable,
# and it makes rollback impossible because the tag has already moved.
TAG=v1.0.1

# --- the RDS instance ---------------------------------------------------------
# The endpoint, copied from the RDS console. A BARE HOSTNAME: no scheme, no
# port, no trailing slash. init-rds.sh rejects the common mistakes.
#
#   right:  lolsuit.abc123xyz.eu-central-1.rds.amazonaws.com
#   wrong:  lolsuit.abc123xyz.eu-central-1.rds.amazonaws.com:3306
#   wrong:  mysql://lolsuit.abc123xyz.eu-central-1.rds.amazonaws.com
DB_HOST=
DB_PORT=3306

# The database inside that instance. RDS creates one for you only if you set an
# "initial database name" when launching it; otherwise create it yourself once
# (init-rds.sh prints the exact command if it cannot find it).
DB_NAME=lolsuit

# The master user from the RDS console works, but a dedicated least-privilege
# account is better - the app only ever needs DML plus the DDL that init.sql
# applies. See prod/README.md, "A least-privilege database user".
DB_USER=lolsuit
# Generate, do not invent:  openssl rand -base64 32
# Avoid / @ " and spaces - RDS rejects some of them in master passwords, and
# they need escaping in connection strings.
DB_PASSWORD=

# There is NO MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD here. That belonged to the MySQL container in
# the development stack; with RDS, AWS owns the master account and the instance
# is administered through the console, the CLI, or a mysql client.

# --- public URL ---------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED. Your real, browser-facing origin - scheme included, no trailing
# slash. Before you attach a domain this is the instance's public DNS name:
#   http://ec2-13-49-1-2.eu-north-1.compute.amazonaws.com
# After: https://lolsuit.example.com
CLIENT_ORIGIN=

# 1 marks the session cookie Secure + SameSite=None, which is correct behind
# HTTPS and REQUIRED once you terminate TLS. The prod compose file defaults it
# to 1.
#
# IMPORTANT: while you are still on plain HTTP via the EC2 public DNS, this
# MUST be 0 - a Secure cookie is never sent over http://, so login will appear
# to succeed and then every subsequent request will be anonymous.
FLASK_SESSION_SECURE=0

# --- password-reset email -----------------------------------------------------
# Leave this alone and the reset link is PRINTED to the server log instead of
# being sent - `docker compose logs server` shows a framed "LolSuit mail" block
# with the link in it. That is the right default for development: the flow is
# fully demonstrable with no mail account anywhere.
MAIL_BACKEND=console

# To actually deliver mail, set MAIL_BACKEND=smtp and fill in the rest.
#
# Brevo (brevo.com) is the recommended relay: 300 emails/day free, no domain
# required, no credit card. Sign up, verify ONE sender address under
# "Senders & IPs" (a plain Gmail address is fine), then copy the login and key
# from "SMTP & API". MAIL_FROM must be that verified address; a display name in
# front of it is fine.
#
# MAIL_BACKEND=smtp
# MAIL_FROM=LolSuit <lol-suit-no-reply@gmail.com>
# SMTP_HOST=smtp-relay.brevo.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_USE_TLS=1
#
# Other relays are a swap of the host and the credentials, nothing else - the
# mail code speaks plain SMTP + STARTTLS on 587 either way:
#
#   Mailjet   SMTP_HOST=in-v3.mailjet.com    (200/day; API key + secret key)
#   Gmail     SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com       (~500/day) - but it needs 2-Step
#             Verification on the account plus a 16-character App Password (the
#             ordinary account password fails with "535 Username and Password
#             not accepted"), it REWRITES the From header to the address you
#             authenticated as, and it sometimes blocks sign-ins coming from a
#             server in another country.
#
# Port 587 matters on EC2: AWS throttles outbound port 25 by default.
# The SMTP password is a credential. It belongs in .env (gitignored), never in
# docker-compose.yml and never in a commit.
# NOTE: with MAIL_BACKEND left at console, password-reset links are never
# emailed - they only appear in `docker compose logs server`, where a real
# user will never see them. Set up a relay below before you tell anyone the
# reset flow works.

# --- how the port is exposed --------------------------------------------------
# Default: port 80 on every interface, i.e. the `web` container IS your public
# server. The EC2 security group must allow inbound 80 (and 443 once you add
# TLS) from 0.0.0.0/0, and 22 from your own IP only.
#
# Running Caddy / host nginx in front for TLS? Then Compose should not own port
# 80. Uncomment both lines and point the proxy at 127.0.0.1:8080:
# WEB_PUBLISH_BIND=127.0.0.1
# WEB_PUBLISH_PORT=8080

# --- capacity -----------------------------------------------------------------
# gunicorn sizing. gthread workers, so threads carry the concurrency; the SSE
# notification stream holds a thread for the life of each subscriber.
# A t3.micro (2 vCPU, 1GB) is fine with the defaults below.
GUNICORN_WORKERS=2
GUNICORN_THREADS=16

# Container memory ceilings. Sized for a 1GB instance; raise on a bigger one.
# There is no DB limit any more - the database is RDS and is sized separately,
# by instance class, in the AWS console.
SERVER_MEMORY_LIMIT=512m
WORKER_MEMORY_LIMIT=512m
WEB_MEMORY_LIMIT=128m

# --- trial timing -------------------------------------------------------------
# Minutes per trial phase. 1440 is a day, which is the real product behaviour.
# Leave it alone in production unless you are running a demo.
PHASE_MINUTES=1440
TICK_SECONDS=15

# --- the bots' brain ----------------------------------------------------------
# 1 = the deterministic offline generator. No credentials, no network calls, no
# per-request cost, fully functional. A legitimate production setting, not a
# fallback - and the default.
BRAIN_FORCE_OFFLINE=1

# For a real model, set BRAIN_FORCE_OFFLINE=0 and pick a provider.
#
#   bedrock - the right answer on EC2. Credentials come from the standard AWS
#             chain, so attach an IAM ROLE to the instance with
#             bedrock:InvokeModel and no key ever touches this file. Region is
#             the one thing the SDK cannot infer.
# LLM_PROVIDER=bedrock
# AWS_REGION=eu-central-1
#
#             LLM_API_KEY does nothing here. Bedrock never reads it, so setting
#             it on this provider looks like credentials and behaves like none:
#             every call fails with "Could not resolve AWS credentials from
#             session" and the bots quietly fall back to the offline generator.
#
#             Without a role, give the chain something it does recognise - a
#             Bedrock API key (Console -> Bedrock -> API keys) is the shortest
#             path, and is a bearer token, not an AWS access key:
# AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK=
#             or a long-lived IAM user, which is the same secret split in two:
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
#
#   gateway - for a box with no AWS identity at all. Something else - an API
#             Gateway route in front of a Lambda, typically - holds the real
#             Bedrock credentials, and this end just POSTs {"prompt": "..."}
#             with an x-api-key header and reads {"text": "..."} back. Needs
#             no SDK, no region and no IAM role, which is the whole point.
#
#             Note what it costs: the endpoint picks the model, ignores
#             max_tokens, has no system turn and no schema enforcement, and
#             caps its own output - about 450 characters in Hebrew. Short
#             lines are unaffected; long filings are asked to be brief and
#             fall back to the offline generator when they still overflow.
# LLM_PROVIDER=gateway
# LLM_ENDPOINT=https://<id>.execute-api.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/prod/suggest
# LLM_API_KEY=
#
#   gemini  - Google's API: one key, no region, no endpoint, and unlike the
#             gateway it DOES enforce a JSON schema, so filings, juror votes
#             and memory rewrites all work. The free tier allows roughly 1,500
#             requests a day, which is several times what this site spends at
#             the default pacing - see the note on TICK_SECONDS above.
#             The key is created at aistudio.google.com/apikey.
# LLM_PROVIDER=gemini
# LLM_API_KEY=
#
#   anthropic - the direct API. LLM_API_KEY is a real secret in a real file on
#               a real server. chmod 600 this file, and prefer AWS Secrets
#               Manager or SSM Parameter Store if you can.
# LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic
# LLM_API_KEY=

# Empty means the provider's default model (see server/app/brain/llm.py).
# The two Claude providers default to Claude Opus 5; gemini defaults to
# gemini-3.7-flash. To trade some quality for cost:
# LLM_MODEL=anthropic.claude-sonnet-5 (bedrock), claude-sonnet-5 (direct),
# or gemini-2.5-flash-lite (gemini, and far cheaper if you leave the free tier).
# LLM_MODEL=

# What the bots may treat as "current" when they file a topical lawsuit.
# Comma-separated, and deliberately operator-set rather than scraped: a human
# decides what the court riffs on, so no real named person can end up as a
# defendant by accident. The season, the weekday and the hour come from the
# clock for free and need nothing here.
#
# TOPICAL_SUBJECTS=הגשם שהגיע מוקדם השנה,שביתת הרכבת,הבחינות של סוף הסמסטר
